// ============================================================================
// DEMO: the INTENDED use of replace_program() -- deferred, DATA-DRIVEN
// runtime polymorphism.
//
// This is the "here's the good pattern" counterpart to the redundant
// self-target no-op handled by replace_program_is_noop() (src/packages/
// core/replace_program.cc) -- a redundant
// `inherit X; create(){replace_program(X);}` on an object that already
// fully has X. This file is a genuine, non-no-op use: a generic
// loader/"shell" object that decides its real class at create() time and
// transforms into it.
//
// Companion fixtures/tests (read these too for the full picture):
//   demo_poly_base.lpc            -- shared,
//     stateless scaffolding every concrete type (and the shell) has
//   demo_poly_{goblin,wolf,ghost}.lpc
//     -- the concrete monster classes this shell can become
//   /single/tests/efuns/replace_program_polymorphism_demo.lpc
//     -- the wired LPC-level test; see its header for exactly what a
//        synchronous LPC test can and cannot observe about the swap
//   src/tests/test_lpc.cc's ReplaceProgramPolymorphicShellDemoLandsForReal
//     -- forces the deferred sweep at the C++ level and proves the FULL
//        landed transformation (chosen type's behavior present, the
//        OTHER candidates' and the shell's own machinery genuinely shed,
//        not just shadowed)
//
// HOW replace_program() ACTUALLY CONSTRAINS THIS PATTERN
// --------------------------------------------------------
// Read this before reusing this shape elsewhere: the driver only allows
// swapping to a program the calling object ALREADY INHERITS (see
// search_inherited() in src/packages/core/replace_program.cc -- targeting
// anything else fails with "program to replace the current with has to
// be inherited"). So a shell that wants to become any ONE of several
// concrete types has to `inherit` ALL of them up front -- the compiled
// PROGRAM genuinely contains every candidate's code; there is no way to
// defer compiling the candidates themselves.
//
// What the pattern actually saves is PER-OBJECT memory, not compile-time
// memory: every individual clone of this shell only ever exists once,
// and the moment create() decides which candidate it really is, the
// OTHER candidates' variables and functions are discarded from THAT
// object (see replace_programs()'s variable-trimming code in
// replace_program.cc -- "move our variables up to the top" / "free the
// rest"). Concretely here: every shell clone starts out carrying 3
// separate hp variables (goblin's, wolf's, and ghost's) and all 3
// classes' functions; the instant the swap lands, 2 of those 3 hp slots
// and every function that came only from the un-chosen candidates are
// gone from that specific object, for good. A mudlib with dozens of
// monster templates and thousands of live spawns saves real memory this
// way even though every template's source was compiled into the shell's
// one shared program.
//
// The runtime decision itself -- WHICH candidate to keep -- is the part
// that couldn't be expressed with a plain `inherit` at all: it comes
// from a spawn table roll, a room's configured monster mix, a save file,
// or (here, for a self-contained example) plain random().
//
// NOTE: unlike the redundant self-target no-op handled by
// replace_program_is_noop() (src/packages/core/replace_program.cc) -- an
// object `inherit`ing X and then calling replace_program(X) on that SAME
// class it already fully has, which the driver now detects as a
// structural no-op and skips -- every replace_program() call below
// discards real content (2 of the 3 candidates' functions and
// variables, plus the shell's own machinery): a genuine structural
// transformation, not a no-op.
// ============================================================================

// Compiling this deliberately emits benign "inherited from both X and Y;
// using the definition in Y" / "Redeclaration of global variable" compiler
// warnings for query_hp()/attack_damage()/hp, since all 3 candidates
// below declare them independently under the same names. That is
// harmless here: nothing calls them THROUGH the shell layer before the
// swap (only after, once ob->prog is unambiguously one specific
// candidate's own program) -- the warnings are the compiler accurately
// describing "3 siblings with the same interface, decision deferred",
// not a mistake to fix.
inherit "/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_goblin";
inherit "/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_wolf";
inherit "/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_ghost";

// In a real mudlib this would be populated from a spawn table, room
// config, or similar external/persisted data -- not a literal here -- but
// the mechanism is identical either way: the choice of WHICH path to
// hand to replace_program() is made at RUNTIME. Keyed by a short species
// name so callers (a spawn system restoring a saved monster type, or the
// test below) can request a specific outcome without knowing the
// underlying file layout.
private mapping candidate_types = ([
  "goblin": "/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_goblin",
  "wolf": "/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_wolf",
  "ghost": "/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_ghost",
]);

// Lives ONLY on this shell layer -- gone entirely once the swap lands:
// replace_programs() doesn't just splice in the chosen candidate, it
// makes ob->prog literally THAT candidate's own compiled program, so
// nothing defined only on the shell layer (this function, and the other
// 2 candidates' own functions/variables) survives. Proves the shell's
// own loader machinery is genuinely SHED, not merely shadowed by an
// override.
string shell_only_marker() { return "shell"; }

// forced_species lets callers (tests, or a mudlib's own deterministic
// spawn logic, e.g. restoring a saved monster type) pin the outcome by
// short name; leaving it unset exercises the real runtime decision via
// random(), same as a live spawn would.
void create(string forced_species) {
  string chosen = forced_species && candidate_types[forced_species];

  if (!chosen) {
    // The actual runtime decision this whole pattern exists for.
    string *species = keys(candidate_types);
    chosen = candidate_types[species[random(sizeof(species))]];
  }

  replace_program(chosen);
}
